WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:10.030
The initial generation of the primary genetic
sequence of a particular organism is called

00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:11.490
de novo sequencing.

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:17.200
A detailed genetic analysis of any organism
is possible only after de novo sequencing

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:19.270
has been performed.

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:23.910
De novo sequencing is typically accomplished
by assembling individual sequence reads into

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:29.930
longer contiguous sequences or correctly ordered
contigs in the absence of a reference sequence.

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:36.330
Historically, de novo sequencing was carried
out using capillary electrophoresis sequencers.

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:41.210
With its long-read lengths and high accuracy,
CE-based sequencing made overlap consensus

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:45.030
assembly the gold-standard technology for
de novo projects.

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:50.379
However, more recently, the high-throughput
capabilities of massively parallel sequencing

00:00:50.379 --> 00:00:54.629
and the development of short-read assemblers
have significantly reduced the time and cost

00:00:54.629 --> 00:00:58.089
associated with sequencing an entire genome.

00:00:58.089 --> 00:01:03.530
During the de novo assembly process, partially
or sometimes fully overlapping reads are assembled

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:05.950
into one or more contigs.

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:11.570
Sets of overlapping or non-overlapping contigs
are joined into one or more scaffolds.

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:17.130
Sets of overlapping or non-overlapping scaffolds
are joined into a single chromosome.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:22.740
In the contig assembly step, reads must overlap
by a minimum number of base pairs, or k-mers,

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:25.280
before they can be mapped together.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:29.979
In the scaffold assembly step, contigs do
not necessarily have to overlap in order to

00:01:29.979 --> 00:01:32.070
be joined together.

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:35.229
This can be attributed to paired-end sequencing.

00:01:35.229 --> 00:01:40.499
In the chromosome assembly step, scaffolds
are joined together in a gap-filling, gap-closing,

00:01:40.499 --> 00:01:43.219
or genome finishing process.

00:01:43.219 --> 00:01:48.359
This final step is difficult and sometimes
impossible to complete using only short-read

00:01:48.359 --> 00:01:49.530
technology.

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:54.969
The presence of repetitive sequences especially
can inhibit gap-filling using only short-reads,

00:01:54.969 --> 00:01:58.490
although some progress is being made in this
area.

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:02.979
Finishing complete chromosomes often require
the use of multiple sequencing technologies

00:02:02.979 --> 00:02:05.749
and hybrid assembly protocols.

00:02:05.749 --> 00:02:10.400
It is common to see short-read technology
combined with long-read technology to generate

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:12.640
fully finished genomes.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:18.030
Employing multiple sequencing technologies
on a per-sample basis can be costly.

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:22.570
There are many de novo assembly algorithms
and software applications available for Next

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:25.460
Generation sequencing projects.

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:30.970
For small genome assembly such as bacterial
scale genomes, tools such as Spades and Geneious

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:32.730
can be useful.

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:37.560
The major factors that determine the required
depth in a de novo genome sequencing study

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:42.710
are the error rate of the sequencing method,
the assembly algorithms used, the repeat complexity

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:47.030
of the particular genome under study and the
read length.

00:02:47.030 --> 00:02:51.370
Genomes that have been sequenced to high depths
by short-read technologies are not necessarily

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:55.900
a substantial improvement in assembly quality
compared with those produced using the earlier

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:59.480
lower-coverage Sanger sequencing technology.

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:04.260
Although the human genome was initially assembled
to high quality with 8–10-fold coverage

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:10.350
using long-read Sanger sequencing, a raw coverage
of approximately 73-fold was required to generate

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:14.710
the first short-read-only assembly of the
giant panda genome that was of lower quality

00:03:14.710 --> 00:03:16.790
than the human genome.

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:22.980
A similarly low coverage, approximately 7.5-fold
dog genome, which is similar in size to that

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:27.810
of the giant panda and was assembled using
Sanger sequencing reads, is more complete

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:31.290
and more contiguous than the giant panda genome.

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:36.030
These differences arise because Sanger sequencing
reads are longer, are derived from larger

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:41.670
insert libraries and can be assembled using
mature assembly algorithms.

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:46.070
High-quality assemblies are now often produced
using hybrid approaches, in which the advantages

00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:51.470
of high-depth, short-read sequencing are complemented
with those of lower-depth but longer-read

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:52.700
sequencing.

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:58.570
In general, low coverage has two principal
effects on analyses and biological interpretation.

00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:04.090
First, it is not possible to resolve whether
an absence of a protein-coding gene, or a

00:04:04.090 --> 00:04:09.620
disruption of its open reading frame, represents
a deficiency of the assembly or a real evolutionary

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:10.950
gene loss.

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:16.160
Second, and perhaps more seriously, low depth
can introduce sequence errors that are in

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:21.220
danger of being mistakenly propagated through
downstream analyses and misdirecting conclusions

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:22.910
of a study.

00:04:22.910 --> 00:04:26.610
To mitigate this possibility, two approaches
are recommended.

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:32.040
First, low-quality bases or sequences that
align poorly against a closely related genome

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:34.690
should be discarded from such analyses.

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:39.400
Second, adjacent bases that have high-quality
scores should also be discarded because they

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:42.400
can contain a high density of residual sequence
errors.

